Chapter: FsSpawnInherits
  cites Foreword chapter FileSystem

   10  the write landed. The FileSystem.Write grant came with the child
       and the servicer elevated on its behalf though it holds no block
       device.
    0  the write did not land: the cap did not travel, or the servicer
       does not work in a spawned process.
 Anything else means the parent read a byte the child never wrote.
 The parent waits for the child instead of yielding once: a servicer
 round-trip is longer than one scheduler turn, and a peek that can
 race the poke reports the boot content of byte 28000, not the verdict.

Section: Entry

  opening : [Concurrent, Console, FileSystem.Write "OK"] Integer
  opening = act
    pid <- process-spawn (\x -> poke-byte 28000 0 (if write-file "OK.TXT" "child data" then 10 else 0))
    let w = process-wait pid
    in peek-byte 28000 0
  end
